he same time  there's some 400 new coal mines planned  is this a realistic goal and how do we  achieve it  i really welcomed the most recent report  of the international energy agency  for the first time aligning with the  goal that we have to  implement of staying well you know below  at or below 1.5 degrees celsius above  pre-industrial standards  and that meant as fat fatty birol  rightly said you know come on guys we  got to go there we got to get out of  fossil fuel and as quickly as possible  um this is where the real action is  i'm glad that business leaders are  stepping up as much as they are  i'm glad that courts are taking actions  against shell for example  and um that shareholders are taking  action against exxon  in shareholder meetings all of this is  contributing and investors  investors in particular have issued a  recent statement  they will not invest if you and you know  if you go the  fossil fuel route but look at what the  g7 countries are doing coming out of  recovery  only 22 percent of the  investment so far coming out of um  covert is for clean energy  only 22 that's not tolerable  what you're touching on there is that it  needs to go deeper doesn't it than uh  sort of cheerful communique at the end  of the g7 there needs to be a lot of  detail  what detail specifically would you like  to see  i want to see the g7 commit  to every country this year  in glasgow having a nationally  determined contribution which is real  worked out measurable and about um  being aligned to the 1.5 degrees so it's  yeah yes it's good to have the long-term  goal of being zero carbon and zero  emissions  by 2050 but  we're more interested in what's going to  happen in 2025 2024  2026 you know measurable just as the  vaccines have to be measurable which is  why  the elders really support the gavi  um kovacs commitment that was made  for a billion by the 1st of september  and 2 billion by mid 20  2022 not saying in a global way we will  vaccinate the whole world  that's kind of rhetoric we need  precision we need  measurement we need real uh commitment  the more the g7 can recognize their  utter  responsibility we have had a year two  years really  of you know devastation around the world  and of course it's worse for developing  countries and it's worse for  the sustainable development goals  progress in developing countries it's  worse for girls  out of school who are driven into early  child marriage it's worse for the  unemployed youth who think there's now  no hope at all  and this has to change the  responsibility is huge  and it's a g7 actual responsibility  leading to a g20 in july  of changing the dynamic in a measurable  way  so we're on course for a safe and more  equitable world  mary robinson it's been a pleasure  talking to you on sky news thank you
 

